<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romagna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romagna is a historical region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>, the Adriatic and the lower Po, now the eastern part of Emilia-Romagna. Its towns, among them Ravenna, Rimini, Forlì, Faenza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesena">Cesena</a>, were held by local lords under papal overlordship until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> and Julius II brought them under direct control in the early 16th century. Part of the Papal States until 1859, it voted in 1860 to join the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and so became part of Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romagna.jpg" alt="Romagna" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Erinaceus · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Romagna_geopolitica.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In alliance with Napoleon III of France, Sardinia defeated Austria in 1859 at Magenta and Solferino and gained Lombardy; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, Modena and the <b>Romagna</b>, while Savoy and Nice were ceded to France as the price of French support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The war of 1859 brought Lombardy by the Armistice of Villafranca and the Treaty of Zurich; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, Modena and the <b>Romagna</b>, while Savoy and Nice were ceded to France by the Treaty of Turin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1500 Cesare Borgia, son of Pope Alexander VI, subdued the lords of the <b>Romagna</b> with French help, and Pope Julius II personally led his troops, recovering Perugia and Bologna in 1506 and besieging Mirandola in 1511.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On 29 June 1797 Napoleon proclaimed the Cisalpine Republic, merging the Transpadane Republic with territories that soon included the Cispadane Republic, <b>Romagna</b>, the Valtellina and the former Venetian cities of Bergamo, Brescia and Crema.</blockquote><a href="/state/cisalpine-republic">Cisalpine Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1499 and 1501 Cesare Borgia, son of Pope Alexander VI, conquered the small lordships of <b>Romagna</b> and the Marche, including Imola, Forlì, Pesaro, Rimini and Faenza, with French and papal troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-romagna">Duchy of Romagna</a></li><li><blockquote>The Montefeltro family, lords of a mountainous area on the borders of <b>Romagna</b> and the Marche, received Urbino as counts in the early 13th century; the register dates the county from 1213.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-urbino">Duchy of Urbino</a></li></ul></section>
